insert into learn_lessons (
  slug, module_slug, position, title, kind, body_markdown, component_key, payload
) values (
  'intro', 'how-donation-happens', 0, 'Why this matters', 'INTRO',
  'Beginner Level gave you the short answer to the question people fear most: death is certified by at least two doctors, and none of them may have anything to do with the transplant. That answer is true, and for a conversation over coffee it is enough.

This Stage is for the next question, the one that comes when someone is really listening:

*How do they actually know?*

A family sitting beside a ventilated patient sees a warm body, a chest that rises and falls, and a heart beating on the monitor. Being told that this person has died contradicts everything their eyes are telling them. That is not ignorance. It is a reasonable response to an unusual situation, and it is one of the biggest obstacles anywhere in the donation pathway.

So this Stage explains how death is determined, at the level of *why* rather than *how to*. It covers both of the ways South Africa recognises: death by neurological criteria (brain death) and death by circulatory criteria. Each makes more sense next to the other, so they are taught together. The Stage then follows a donation from the moment someone first thinks of it to the moment organs and tissue are recovered, and names the organisations involved along the way.

One boundary holds throughout. **Nothing here is a procedure.** You will learn what the tests are and why they prove what they prove. You will not learn to perform them, and no clinical values are taught. The aim is to be able to explain, accurately, why a death is final.

Start from the definition, which is more precise than most people expect:

> Death is the irreversible loss of the capacity for consciousness together with the irreversible loss of the capacity to breathe.

It does not mention the heart. Keep that in mind through everything that follows.', NULL, NULL
) on conflict (module_slug, slug) do update set
  position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, kind = excluded.kind,
  body_markdown = excluded.body_markdown, component_key = excluded.component_key,
  payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();

insert into learn_lessons (
  slug, module_slug, position, title, kind, body_markdown, component_key, payload
) values (
  'determining-death', 'how-donation-happens', 1, 'Determining death', 'PRIMARY',
  '## Two ways, one definition

South Africa recognises two ways of determining that someone has died:

- **By neurological criteria**, usually called **brain death**. The brain, including the brainstem, has permanently stopped working, while a ventilator keeps oxygen moving through the body.
- **By circulatory criteria**, or **circulatory death**. The heart has stopped, and circulation and breathing have been absent for long enough that they will not return.

These are not two kinds of death, and neither is a stage on the way to dying. They are two routes to establishing the same fact: the capacity for consciousness and the capacity to breathe have both been lost, irreversibly. Which route applies depends on the circumstances, and in particular on whether a ventilator is involved.

The tests in this lesson come from the *South African guidelines on the determination of death*, written for the Critical Care Society of Southern Africa and published in 2021 in the peer-reviewed *Southern African Journal of Critical Care*. It is South African guidance, written for South African hospitals, and the guideline itself says that, provided every precondition is met, death can be determined with complete certainty.

## Brain death is not a coma

This is the distinction you will be asked to explain most often, and it is the one most often wrong in films and news reports.

**A coma** is profound unconsciousness. The brain is badly injured, but it has not lost all of its function. The brainstem, the part at the base of the brain that controls breathing and the most basic reflexes, still works. People do recover from comas, which is exactly why a coma must never be confused with death.

**Brain death** is the complete and irreversible loss of all brain function, including the brainstem. There is nothing left to recover. The guidelines put it plainly: there is no documented case of anyone who met the preconditions and criteria for brain death ever regaining any brain function.

**So why is the heart still beating?** Because a ventilator is supplying the oxygen that a person who is brain dead can no longer take in for themselves, and with that oxygen the heart can go on beating for a time after death. The rise and fall of the chest is the ventilator''s work too. What the family sees at the bedside is the machine, not a sign of life.

## Before any test begins

The most common challenge to brain death is a fair one: *how do you know they aren''t just deeply sedated, or very cold?* The answer is that nobody tests for brain death until those possibilities have been ruled out. Several things can make a living brain look, temporarily, as if it has stopped working. The guidelines require each of them to be excluded first:

- **There must be a known cause.** An established injury, such as a severe head injury, a major bleed in the brain or a prolonged loss of oxygen, that is capable of destroying the whole brain permanently. Testing does not start on a patient whose unresponsiveness has no explanation.
- **Body temperature must be normal.** A very cold body can suppress brain function in a way that recovers on warming.
- **Blood pressure must be adequate.** A brain that is not receiving enough blood can stop responding without having died.
- **Sedatives and other drugs that depress the brain must be excluded.** Many patients in intensive care have been given them, so there must be time for them to clear, or proof that they are not having an effect.
- **Severe chemical imbalances in the blood must be corrected.** Some can dull or switch off brain responses on their own.

This step is what makes the tests mean anything. If every condition that could *mimic* brain death has been ruled out, and the brain still shows no function, then the only remaining explanation is the injury itself, and the injury is one that cannot be undone.

## The three-step test

Once the preconditions are met, the test has three parts. Each one closes off a different possibility.

**1. Coma.** The patient is completely unresponsive. Strong pressure produces no grimace and no movement of the face. Some movements can still come from the spinal cord, which works without the brain, and doctors are trained to tell these reflexes apart from a response. Any response that comes from the brain rules out brain death.

**2. No brainstem reflexes, checked one pathway at a time.** The brainstem carries a set of automatic reflexes that still work in a deep coma. Each one tests a different set of nerves running through the brainstem, so checking them one by one tests the brainstem as a whole, not just one spot:

- a bright light shone into each eye, to see whether the pupils react;
- the surface of the eye (the cornea) touched, to see whether the eye blinks;
- firm pressure just above the eye, to see whether the face grimaces;
- ice-cold water run into each ear, which in a working brainstem makes the eyes move;
- the back of the throat touched, to see whether the patient gags;
- the airway deep in the chest stimulated, to see whether the patient coughs.

If even one of these reflexes is present, the brainstem is still working and the patient is not brain dead.

**3. The apnoea test.** *Apnoea* means the absence of breathing. Because the loss of the capacity to breathe is part of the definition of death, it is tested directly rather than assumed. Under controlled conditions, the patient is disconnected from the ventilator while oxygen continues to be supplied, and the doctors watch to see whether the body ever tries to take a breath. As the body goes without breathing, the waste gas that normally drives us to breathe builds up in the blood. It is the strongest signal to breathe there is. If no breath comes, the breathing centre in the brainstem has stopped working.

Together the three steps rule out every alternative. The preconditions exclude the conditions that could mimic death. The coma and brainstem tests show that no part of the brain is responding. The apnoea test shows that the one function the body cannot live without has gone. That is why a completed determination is final.

## Who makes the determination

Brain death is determined by **at least two doctors**. At least one of them must have been practising for **five years or more** since registering, and **none of them may be involved in the transplant**. The guidelines recommend that they examine the patient together, and strongly advocate that the apnoea test be done once, by both doctors at the same time.

The time of death is the time at which the determination is complete, with every required test done. It is not the moment the ventilator is later switched off, and it is not the moment donation is discussed.

## Circulatory death

Circulatory death is determined differently, because the situation is different. Here the heart has stopped.

Death can only be determined this way in one of three situations: trying to restart the heart would be inappropriate, attempts to restart it have failed, or treatment aimed at keeping the patient alive has been withdrawn. In the donation setting it is the third. The guideline lists the reasons treatment may be withdrawn: it can no longer benefit the patient and continuing it is not in their best interests, or the patient refused it in an advance directive, or their legal decision-maker has done so on their behalf.

Once the heart has stopped, the doctor observes the patient for at least **five minutes**, during which there must be a **continuous** absence of circulation and of breathing. At the end of that period the doctor confirms that the pupils do not react to light and that there is no movement in response to pressure above the eye. The time of death is the moment those criteria are met.

**Why the wait exists.** A heart that has stopped can, very rarely, start again on its own in the first minutes. So the guideline sets five continuous minutes as the period that establishes the stopping is irreversible, and adds a rule: if circulation or breathing returns at any point, the five minutes start again from zero. The wait is there to make certain. It is not a countdown run for the transplant team''s benefit. Without the reason the wait can look arbitrary, or worse, so knowing it is what lets you answer the question honestly.

Two more rules close the gap people worry about. Once death has been confirmed, nothing may be done that could restore blood flow to the brain. And where organs are to be donated, a **second doctor** must also certify the death: as with brain death, at least one of the two must have more than five years'' experience, and neither may be involved with the transplant team.

One more point matters here, and later Stages return to it in depth. In donation after circulatory death, nothing starts until a decision to withdraw treatment has been made **for the patient''s own reasons**, by the team caring for them, independently of any question of donation. Donation is only considered once that decision stands on its own.

## Putting the two side by side

Brain death is determined by showing that the brain has permanently stopped working, while a ventilator keeps the heart beating. The organs go on receiving blood and oxygen after death, and that is what makes **donation after brain death (DBD)** possible.

Circulatory death is determined by showing that the circulation has permanently stopped. From the moment the heart stops, the organs are without blood. That is why **donation after circulatory death (DCD)** runs under much greater time pressure, and why what can be donated depends on how quickly recovery can follow.

Both determinations are careful, rule-bound and done by doctors who have nothing to do with any transplant. Each establishes, by its own route, that the death cannot be reversed.', NULL, NULL
) on conflict (module_slug, slug) do update set
  position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, kind = excluded.kind,
  body_markdown = excluded.body_markdown, component_key = excluded.component_key,
  payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();

insert into learn_lessons (
  slug, module_slug, position, title, kind, body_markdown, component_key, payload
) values (
  'the-process-end-to-end', 'how-donation-happens', 2, 'The process end to end', 'PRIMARY',
  'A donation is not one decision at one moment. It is a chain of steps, run by different people over many hours, and it can break at almost any link. This lesson walks the chain in order.

## 1. Identification: someone has to think of it

Organ donation can only happen in a hospital, in practice an intensive care unit, from a patient on a ventilator. So the chain starts with the doctors and nurses already caring for that patient. **Identifying a possible donor is the treating team''s responsibility**, as part of ordinary end-of-life care. Nobody else is in the room to do it.

The guidance used by South African transplant coordinators names three situations that should prompt the thought, for any patient on a ventilator:

- a decision has been made to test for brain death;
- the patient has a catastrophic brain injury that sedation cannot explain, and there has been time to judge the outlook;
- the team intends to discuss withdrawing life-sustaining treatment, and death is expected to follow.

None of these is a decision to donate. Each is simply a point at which donation might be possible, and at which someone should ask.

## 2. Referral: early, and before the family is asked

At that point the treating team contacts a **transplant coordinator**, or a **tissue donation coordinator** where only tissue is possible. The South African guidelines on the determination of death set the timing: the assessment of donation potential, with the coordinator, should happen when the team decides to test for brain death or to begin discussing withdrawal of treatment with the family. In other words, **before end-of-life matters are raised with the family**, not after. The same guidelines treat exploring a patient''s wishes about donation as a standard part of end-of-life care.

Timing matters for two reasons:

- **The coordinator can find out whether donation is possible at all.** Screening often shows that it is not. Finding that out early means the family is never asked about something that cannot happen.
- **Support takes time to arrange.** An early call lets the coordinator plan who will be involved and when, so the family is not kept waiting later.

**There is no legal duty to refer.** The steps that follow a referral are tightly regulated, but whether the call is made at all depends on a clinician choosing to make it. So a donation that was possible can be lost without anyone ever deciding against it, simply because nobody asked. (Draft regulations published for comment in September 2026 would change this for hospitals that carry out donation. They are not yet law, and Stage 3 covers them.)

## 3. Determination of death

Death is determined by one of the two routes in the previous lesson: by neurological criteria, for a patient on a ventilator, or by circulatory criteria, after treatment has been withdrawn. Either way, the doctors who determine it have nothing to do with any transplant.

## 4. The family is told, and only then asked

The family is first told that their relative has died, and is given time to understand and accept it. **Donation is not raised in the same conversation.** It comes later, and it is usually raised by the coordinator rather than by the doctor who delivered the news.

This separation is called **decoupling**. It means a family is never asked to absorb a death and make a donation decision in the same breath, and never hears the news of a death as a request. Stage 2 covers the conversation itself, and who has the right to consent.

## 5. Consent and authorisation

Consent comes from the family, in an order set by law. Where the death was unnatural, such as a road accident, the Forensic Pathology Service must also authorise what may be recovered, and the hospital gives its own written authorisation. Stages 2 and 3 cover each of these.

## 6. Donor management

After brain death, the body begins to deteriorate, because the brain no longer regulates things such as temperature. Care therefore continues after death, and it is active: breathing, circulation, temperature and fluids are all managed. **Active management increases the number of organs that can be recovered and improves how well recipients do.** It is treatment with a purpose, not holding care.

Families can usually stay with their relative during this time, up to the point of transfer to theatre.

## 7. Recovery

Organs are recovered in an operating theatre, as a surgical procedure performed with the same care and respect as any other operation. Tissue is recovered by trained tissue recovery technicians. Afterwards the body is restored to its original appearance.

## 8. Allocation

Organs are matched to recipients. Allocation is a matching process, not a queue in which the person who has waited longest goes first.

## Two organ pathways: DBD and DCD

Most deceased organ donation follows one of two pathways. They differ at almost every step.

**Donation after brain death (DBD).** The patient is on a ventilator, and death is determined by neurological criteria. Because the ventilator keeps the heart beating, the organs go on receiving blood after death. There is time for donor management and for recovery to be planned. The heart, lungs, liver, kidneys and pancreas can all be considered, as well as tissue.

**Donation after circulatory death (DCD).** The team caring for the patient has made an independent decision, in line with palliative care, to withdraw life-sustaining treatment. Only then is donation considered. If the family consents, support continues while the retrieval is prepared, and then treatment is withdrawn with two doctors on hand to certify death. Nothing is done to hasten the dying. Once death has been determined, recovery has to begin as soon as possible, because the organs are damaged quickly once they have no blood supply. So what can be donated depends on how quickly that happens.

Sometimes the heart does not stop within the time the organs can tolerate. The recovery team then stands down, the patient''s palliative care continues with the treating team, and the family is told that donation was not possible. The Western Cape''s 2025 donation policy, which sets out these steps, describes DCD as still relatively unfamiliar in South Africa, and most deceased organ donation here follows the DBD pathway.

## Organs and tissue: two different pathways

**Organs** have to keep working. They can only come from a DBD or DCD donor, in hospital, and must be transplanted within hours. They are not stored.

**Tissue**, such as corneas, bone, skin and heart valves, does not have to keep working in the same way. It can be donated irrespective of the manner of death, it can be recovered hours or even days after death, including after the body has been moved to a mortuary, and it does not need a ventilator at all. Tissue is processed and stored by a **tissue bank**, so it does not have to be matched to a recipient that day.

That is why far more people can donate tissue than can donate organs.

## Who''s who

- **Organ Donor Foundation (ODF).** A non-profit founded in 1988 as the national umbrella body for promoting organ and tissue donation. It runs awareness campaigns and the donor register. It is not a hospital and plays no part in decisions at the bedside.
- **Southern African Transplantation Society (SATS).** The professional society for transplantation in the region. With SATCS it reports South Africa''s national donation and transplant figures, and it convened the workshop behind the national strategy roadmap for organ and tissue donation.
- **South African Transplant Coordinators Society (SATCS).** Founded in 2017 as a special-interest group of SATS, to educate, develop and support transplant coordinators. It publishes the *Organ and Tissue Donation Reference File*, known as the Red File, the coordinators'' own reference.
- **Transplant coordinators.** Usually specialist nurses. They are the key point of contact between the bereaved family, the clinical team and the transplant team: they take the referral, screen the potential donor, oversee donor management, approach the family about donation and coordinate everything from referral to recovery.
- **Tissue banks.** Organisations that recover, process, store and supply donated tissue. South Africa''s include cornea and eye banks, a heart valve bank and a multi-tissue bank for bone, skin and corneas.
- **Transplant centres.** The hospital units, public and private, where transplants are performed. The SATS roadmap counts 21 transplant centres, including the tissue banks above, and notes that they are concentrated in large cities in the wealthier provinces. Stage 4 returns to what that means for fairness.', NULL, NULL
) on conflict (module_slug, slug) do update set
  position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, kind = excluded.kind,
  body_markdown = excluded.body_markdown, component_key = excluded.component_key,
  payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();

insert into learn_lessons (
  slug, module_slug, position, title, kind, body_markdown, component_key, payload
) values (
  'takeaways', 'how-donation-happens', 3, 'Key takeaways', 'TAKEAWAYS',
  '- **Death is the irreversible loss of the capacity for consciousness and of the capacity to breathe.** South Africa recognises two ways of determining it: by neurological criteria (brain death) and by circulatory criteria (circulatory death).

- **Brain death is not a coma.** People recover from comas. There is no documented case of anyone who met the preconditions and criteria for brain death regaining any brain function. The heart can keep beating after brain death only because a ventilator is supplying oxygen.

- **Nothing is tested until the look-alikes are ruled out.** There must be a known, irreversible cause, a normal temperature, adequate blood pressure, no sedative effect and no severe chemical imbalance. That is the answer to "how do you know they aren''t just sedated, or cold?"

- **The brain-death test has three steps:** coma, then no brainstem reflexes (checked one pathway at a time), then the apnoea test, which shows the body never tries to breathe.

- **Circulatory death needs five continuous minutes** without circulation or breathing, then no pupil response and no movement to pressure. If anything returns during the five minutes, the count restarts. The wait exists because a stopped heart can, very rarely, restart on its own in the first minutes.

- **The doctors who determine death have nothing to do with the transplant.** At least two of them where organs are to be donated, at least one with five or more years'' experience.

- **Referral should happen early, before the family is approached, and it is not a legal duty.** A donation can be lost simply because nobody made the call.

- **DBD and DCD differ at almost every step.** In DCD, donation is considered only after an independent decision to withdraw treatment, nothing is done to hasten death, and if the heart does not stop in time, donation stands down and palliative care continues.

- **Tissue is not organs.** Tissue can be donated irrespective of the manner of death, hours or days later, without a ventilator, and it is stored by tissue banks. That is why far more people can donate tissue.', NULL, NULL
) on conflict (module_slug, slug) do update set
  position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, kind = excluded.kind,
  body_markdown = excluded.body_markdown, component_key = excluded.component_key,
