insert into learn_lessons (
  slug, module_slug, position, title, kind, body_markdown, component_key, payload
) values (
  'intro', 'sa-legal-framework', 0, 'Why this matters', 'INTRO',
  'Advocates get asked legal questions all the time. *Is it legal to sell a kidney? Who certifies death? Does it cost the family anything? Has the law changed?*

Several widely repeated "legal facts" about donation in South Africa are wrong. Some are out of date, some belong to an Act repealed in 2003, and some were never law at all. This Stage states what the law actually says, and marks the places where common material gets it wrong.

Before starting, learn to separate three kinds of statement, because they get tangled together whenever donation is discussed:

- **Legal requirement:** what the law demands. *At least two doctors must establish death, and neither may take part in the transplant.*
- **Clinical guidance:** what good practice recommends. *The two doctors should ideally test together.*
- **Practice:** what actually happens. *Hospitals approach the family in every case, and the family is not billed for the donation.*

All three can be true at once, and they are not the same kind of true. When you speak publicly, say which one you are giving.

One caution stands throughout. A course is not legal advice, and law changes: new regulations were published for comment as recently as September 2026. If a point here is ever load-bearing for a real decision, check the current text, and if you are asked something this Stage does not cover, refer the question rather than reconstructing an answer.', NULL, NULL
) on conflict (module_slug, slug) do update set
  position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, kind = excluded.kind,
  body_markdown = excluded.body_markdown, component_key = excluded.component_key,
  payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();

insert into learn_lessons (
  slug, module_slug, position, title, kind, body_markdown, component_key, payload
) values (
  'the-law-and-the-regulations', 'sa-legal-framework', 1, 'The law and the regulations', 'PRIMARY',
  '## Two levels: the Act and its regulations

The governing law is **Chapter 8 of the National Health Act 61 of 2003**. It replaced the Human Tissue Act of 1983, which was repealed.

Much of the operational detail does not sit in the Act itself. It sits one level down, in the **regulations** made under it: chiefly the *Regulations regarding the general control of human bodies, tissue, blood, blood products and gametes*, published as Government Notice R180 on 2 March 2012. Citing "Chapter 8" for a rule that actually lives in the regulations is a common and avoidable error.

Two definitions in the Act are worth knowing exactly:

- **"Death" means brain death.** That is the Act''s own definition in section 1. Donation after circulatory death is nonetheless practised in South Africa, under provincial policy and the national determination-of-death guidelines, and a 2024 article in the *South African Journal of Bioethics and Law* argues that it is lawful and consistent with Chapter 8. If someone quotes section 1 at you, that is the answer: the question has been asked, and addressed.
- **"Tissue" includes an organ.** So wherever the Act speaks of tissue, it includes kidneys, livers, hearts and lungs.

## Establishing death: Regulation 9

The rule that answers the most common public fear is **Regulation 9** of the 2012 regulations, headed *Establishment of death*. In its own words:

> The death of a person concerned shall be established by at least two medical practitioners, one of whom shall have been practising as a medical practitioner for at least five years after the date on which she or he was registered as a medical practitioner, and none of those medical practitioners shall transplant tissue removed from that person into a living person or take part in such transplantation.

Three things follow:

- It is **at least** two doctors, not exactly two.
- The five years run from **registration**, and apply to one of them.
- The bar is written as an **activity**, not a job title: the doctors who establish death may not transplant the tissue or take part in the transplant. That independence is the whole point of the rule.

Some donation material adds that neither doctor may be an intern. The regulation says no such thing.

**The one exception.** Where the tissue is **eye tissue**, death is deemed established by the issuing of an ordinary death certificate by a doctor. Corneal donation does not need the two-practitioner procedure, which is part of why it is so much simpler to arrange than organ donation.

## Who else must say yes

Consent comes from the person or their family under **section 62**, as Stage 2 set out. Two further authorisations can be required.

**The hospital.** Recovery needs written authorisation from the **medical practitioner in charge of clinical services** at the hospital, or a doctor they authorise. *Medical superintendent* is language from the repealed Human Tissue Act. It still circulates in donation material and it is out of date.

**The Forensic Pathology Service, after an unnatural death.** Where a death is unnatural, such as a road accident, a post-mortem is needed to determine the cause of death, and the Forensic Pathology Service must authorise what may be recovered without compromising that examination. Family consent alone is not enough. Donation is still possible; it involves one more authority saying yes. Older material names the *district surgeon* or *state pathologist*. That office no longer performs this function, and using those terms in public dates the speaker.

## What the law prohibits

**Trade and reward.** Under **section 60**, it is an offence for a donor to receive any financial or other reward for a donation, except reimbursement of reasonable costs incurred to provide it, and an offence to sell or trade in tissue except as Chapter 8 provides. The penalty is a fine, imprisonment of up to five years, or both. This applies to living donors exactly as it does after death.

**Transplants for non-citizens.** Under **section 61(3)**, an organ may not be transplanted into a person who is not a South African citizen or permanent resident without the Minister''s written authorisation. It answers a question advocates are often asked in a suspicious tone: South African organs cannot simply go to foreign patients who can pay.

**Allocation by the rules.** Section 61 also requires organs from deceased donors to be used and allocated only as prescribed, not at any one clinician''s discretion.

## What the law does not say

**Costs.** "The donation costs the family nothing" is true. The Red File states that the donor''s medical aid, estate and next of kin are not responsible for medical costs incurred once the donor has been declared brain dead and consent has been given, nor for any costs of tissue donation, and the Organ Donor Foundation and tissue banks say the same. But it is **practice, not statute**: neither the Act nor the 2012 regulations allocate the costs of donation. The family remains responsible for the care the patient received before death, as with any admission. Say it as practice, never as *"the law says your medical aid can''t be charged"*.

**Referral.** There is **no legal duty to refer** a potential donor. Everything after the referral is tightly regulated; whether it happens at all is not.

## Recent developments

**A Ministerial Advisory Committee, October 2024.** On 4 October 2024 the Minister of Health established the **Ministerial Advisory Committee on Organ Transplantation** (Government Notice 5360), under the Act''s power to appoint advisory committees. Its mandate is to advise the Minister on all matters related to organ transplantation. Its listed functions include advising on transplants involving unrelated donors and non-citizens, proposing which units may perform transplants, monitoring unethical behaviour, and reviewing transplant units'' annual reports. The notice frames it partly as a safeguard against organ trafficking and the abuse of potential donors. Its nine members are a Department of Health representative, a bioethicist and seven nephrologists, appointed for five-year terms.

**Draft transplant regulations, September 2026.** On 4 September 2026 the Department of Health published draft Regulations on Organ Transplantation (Government Notice 7879) for public comment, with comments due within three months. **They are a draft and not yet law.** As published, they would among other things:

- require hospitals that carry out donation to designate staff who must **identify and refer potential donors**, which would create the referral duty that does not exist today;
- require every referral, donation and transplant to be recorded, and reported to the national Department of Health;
- require **licensing** of transplant units;
- prohibit organ allocation from taking into account a deceased donor''s **race, religion, culture, language, gender, sexual orientation, disability** or any other aspect of their life that has no bearing on the organ itself;
- write down the requirement, followed in practice today, that a living donor who is not biologically related to the recipient needs the **Minister''s approval**;
- make whoever recovers the organs responsible for restoring the appearance of the donor''s body.

When you mention them, say "draft regulations published for comment in September 2026". Do not say "the law now requires". Check whether they have been finalised before relying on any of them.

**A correction worth knowing.** Some donation material describes a 2025 amendment to the regulations as a donation development. The 2025 amendment it points to changed the regulations on notifiable medical conditions, which govern disease surveillance, not donation. The genuine recent developments are the two above.', NULL, NULL
) on conflict (module_slug, slug) do update set
  position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, kind = excluded.kind,
  body_markdown = excluded.body_markdown, component_key = excluded.component_key,
  payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();

insert into learn_lessons (
  slug, module_slug, position, title, kind, body_markdown, component_key, payload
) values (
  'takeaways', 'sa-legal-framework', 2, 'Key takeaways', 'TAKEAWAYS',
  '- **Chapter 8 of the National Health Act 61 of 2003 is the governing law**, and much of the detail sits in the regulations made under it, chiefly GN R180 of 2 March 2012. Cite the right one.

- **Separate the three kinds of statement:** legal requirement, clinical guidance and practice. Say which one you are giving.

- **Regulation 9 sets the two-doctor rule:** at least two doctors, one practising for five years or more since registration, and none of them may take part in the transplant. For eye tissue, an ordinary death certificate suffices. The "no interns" rule is not in the regulation.

- **Beyond the family''s consent, two more authorisations can apply:** the hospital''s medical practitioner in charge of clinical services, and, after an unnatural death, the Forensic Pathology Service. "Medical superintendent" and "district surgeon" are out of date.

- **Trade is a crime.** Section 60 makes it an offence to sell or trade in tissue, organs included, or for a donor to be rewarded beyond reasonable costs: up to five years'' imprisonment.

- **A non-citizen or non-permanent-resident needs the Minister''s written authorisation** to receive an organ (section 61(3)).

- **"It costs the family nothing" is practice, not statute.** Neither the Act nor the regulations allocate donation costs. There is also no legal duty to refer.

- **Two real recent developments:** the Ministerial Advisory Committee on Organ Transplantation (October 2024), and draft Regulations on Organ Transplantation (September 2026), which are **not yet law**.', NULL, NULL
) on conflict (module_slug, slug) do update set
  position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, kind = excluded.kind,
  body_markdown = excluded.body_markdown, component_key = excluded.component_key,
  payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();

